WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.880
In January 2022, Bloomberg reported that the&nbsp;
Federal Trade Commission is investigating Meta

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:17.280
for supposedly anti-competitive conduct concerning&nbsp;
Meta's Oculus headset and app store. If true,

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.800
this is another example of antitrust overreach.&nbsp;
Virtual reality and the metaverse are in their

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:29.217
developmental stages. Markets are still sorting out&nbsp;
what services and technologies customers will want.

00:00:29.217 --> 00:00:34.560
Yet, amazingly, the investigators apparently&nbsp;
believe that they know what prices should be

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:40.480
and how devices and services should be sold. Such&nbsp;
overreach can severely hamper the metaverse's

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:47.440
growth and development. One concern is about&nbsp;
Oculus's relatively low price. This is misguided.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:53.760
Sony's headset is about the same price. Even if the&nbsp;
Oculus price is below cost, this is common in the

00:00:53.760 --> 00:01:00.480
gaming industry, where companies subsidize consoles&nbsp;
to get customers into the ecosystem. The regulators

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.120
should put their investigative resources&nbsp;
elsewhere and let customers drive the metaverse.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.440
What do you think? Is Meta damaging&nbsp;
competition in the metaverse?

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:16.560
Let us know in our poll, and let us know&nbsp;
what other topics you'd like our scholars

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:23.600
to cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and&nbsp;
subscribe to more research and videos from AEI.

